\par\par \b \uc2 SNB Move to Leave Hungary Unfazed, ING Says -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,447 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2015\par 13:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 1223 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--The \uc2 Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's timing of its household foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  mortgage move was extremely lucky as this morning's \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  decison to scrap its EUR/CHF floor will likely have only a very limited effect on Hungary, ING economist Andras Balatoni says. Now Hungary basically has no risky foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure, with banks having already covered their exposure in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and small enterprises have also had the opportunity to exchange their \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans to forints thanks to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's lending program. Amid the current very low inflationary environment the franc's rise could even be good for Hungary's economy, Mr. Balatoni adds. (Veronika.gulyas@wsj.com; @VeronikaGulyas1) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 1221 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--The Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy changes Thursday make a negative main repo rate in Sweden more likely, says Carl Hammer at SEB bank in Stockholm. Before Thursday, SEB had already expected a Riksbank rate cut to -0.1% from zero in February. The higher probability of such an outcome should weigh on SEK, Mr. Hammer says. EUR/SEK trades at 9.48. (charles.duxbury@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1216 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 's policy changes Thursday have raised the likelihood that the Swedish Riksbank will need to do more than just postpone rate hikes at its February meeting, says Anna Raman at Nykredit markets. "With negative monetary policy rates across Europe and with the ECB becoming the most aggressive larger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the pressure on the Riksbank to ease further has increased," she says. EUR/SEK trades at 9.4795. (charles.duxbury@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7:15 EST - Employment among Brazilian industries dropped in November, the 8th-straight monthly decline, as industrial activity remains the weakest part of the struggling economy. Worker levels fell 0.4% on-month and are down 3.1% through November for 2014. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1213 GMT The decision by Switzerland Thursday to end its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cap "won't have any significant" effect on Austria's debt management, the Alpine country's Treasury Head Martha Oberndorfer says Thursday. She adds that the Treasury doesn't hold any foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risks. The move may be positive for the Austrian economy, especially for the Western part of the country, near the Swiss border, she adds. Switzerland's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said earlier Thursday that it would no longer cap the Swiss franc at 1.20 to the euro, a policy put in place in 2011 to head off deflation and help exporters. (Nicole.lundeen@wsj.com; @nicole_lundeen) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1211 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--The Czech koruna is regaining its title as central European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  safe haven following sharp drops on neighboring \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after today's Swiss \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  announcement, says Jan Vejmelek, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Komercni Banka\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The koruna is bolstered thanks to previous \f28 \uc2 \f2 Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves that helped the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Czech Republic\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  avoid deflation. In Nov. '13 the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, weakening the koruna over 5% to the euro, which boosted inflation and prevented a deflationary spiral. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  established a koruna trading range and that should hold in coming days, Mr. Vejmelek says. Risks mainly come ahead of the EBC's meeting next week where it might launch QE, and ahead of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Feb. 5 meeting where it'll should announce a weaker inflation outlook but likely keep monetary policy unchanged, he says. EUR/CZK trades 0.5% firmer on the day against the euro at 27.91. (sean.carney@dowjones.com; @SeanCarneyWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1207 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Thailand's household debt is likely to rise to as high as 88%-89% of GDP in 2015, according to Kasikorn Research Center. The expected rise in household debt should be driven by Thailand's continued economic recovery as well as government policies to lower living costs and boost purchasing power, Kasikorn said. According to the research house's estimates, the country's household debt is likely to reach 85.5% of GDP in 2014 from 82.3% in 2013. (wilawan.watcharasakwet@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: FLS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1206 GMT \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Lufthansa AG\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Swiss Air Lines "will face a challenge to match competitor fares against a now higher cost base," RBC Capital says in a note. The Swiss franc surged more than 20% against the euro Thursday after Switzerland's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  scrapped a long-standing cap on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . RBC analyst Damien Brewer sees the airline facing higher local wage, airport and other service costs. Swiss Air generated around 26% of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lufthansa\f28 \uc2 \f2 's operating result in the first nine months last year, RBC says. One upside--any remaining profit Swiss Air generates will translate to more money for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lufthansa\f28 \uc2 \f2  when the money is brought back to Germany. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Lufthansa\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares are flat at EUR14.58. (robert.wall@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1157 GMT The current oil price slump may trigger more bond issuance, both conventional and Islamic, from the Persian Gulf region, says a chief investment officer at Franklin Templeton. "One of the silver linings really of the lower oil prices is more issuance," says Mohiedinne Kronfol. Several governments across the Gulf region in their 2015 budgets have signaled they won't cut spending at a time when lower oil prices could curb their revenues. "If you do run budget deficits you will need to finance that," says Mr. Kronfol. He adds, however, oil prices will likely not remain at the current depressed levels. (Nicolas.parasie@wsj.com; Twitter: @NicolasParasie) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1149 GMT Austria's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Erste Group Bank AG\f28 \uc2 \f2  is down over 7% in Prague and more than 6% in Vienna on concerns the bank faces additional risks to Swiss franc exposure in some CEE markets where it operates, namely in Hungary. The fall comes after the Swiss \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  abandoned its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  floor against the euro. Timothy Ash, chief emerging markets economist at Standard Bank, says it's unlikely losses from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  loans in Hungary will be borne by households and "the hope is the [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Hungarian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ] hedged itself by holding the necessary CHF to cover such an \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  move." Should that not be the case, a "not insubstantial hit to the NBH P&L might create concerns over further revisions to the existing program to spread the burden of today's move." (sean.carney@dowjones.com; @SeanCarneyWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 6:47 EST - US stock-index futures are strongly lower today as the Swiss \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's moves on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and interest-rate fronts seem to have merely ratcheted up investor concerns. Policy makers scrapped their minimum exchange rate for the franc and slashed its 3-month Libor target, in the process sending the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  screaming higher (the dollar is down 14% against it at CHF0.8768). Dow-industrial futures, which were up nearly 100 points two hours ago, when the announcements were made, are now down 75. The S&P 500 is off 11. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1144 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Qatar, Saudi Arabia and Abu Dhabi are well-positioned to continue with massive government-led development plans, but a combination of high construction costs and lower oil prices may bring about a review of spending priorities, consultancy \f28 \uc2 \f2 EC Harris\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. Construction costs in Qatar are the highest in the Middle East, according to a new \f28 \uc2 \f2 EC Harris\f28 \uc2 \f2  report, followed by the United Arab Emirates and Saudi Arabia. They are still relatively low compared with the rest of the world, however, and these countries have been shielded from fluctuations in global commodity prices and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by their pegs to the U.S. dollar, the report says. At the same time, falling oil prices could prompt a fresh review of big projects. (asa.fitch@wsj.com; Twitter: @wsjmideast) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 15, 2015 07:23 ET (12:23 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150115eb1f001iv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
