\par\par \b \uc2 Macro Horizons: Welcome Signs of Easing in Eurozone, Doubts On China\b0\par\par\uc2 1,427 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2014\par 13:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Michael J. Casey and Alen Mattich\par \par \uc2 Macro Horizons covers the main macroeconomic and policy news events affecting \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fixed income and equity markets around the world, as selected by editors in New York, London and Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 WRAP: Markets have received some rare upbeat news out of the Eurozone, where the ferocious credit squeeze seems to be relenting. With new \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity measures coming in and its president, \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2 , promising more if necessary, all while the euro is weakening, the momentum in monetary conditions could for the first time in years be headed in a more constructive direction to promote badly needed growth. Offsetting that positive news: the direction of monetary policy out of China could be thrown in doubt if Chinese leaders seeking to oust reform-minded governor of the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 , \uc2 Zhou Xiaochuan\uc2 , have their way. Investors are figuring how to play that news, reported exclusively by The Wall Street Journal: do they buy on the short-term expectation that Mr. Zhou's departure would signal easier policy, or sell on the grounds that a reformer is being pushed aside and the longer-term risk to sustainable economic growth will be exacerbated. (AM, MC)\par \par \uc2 EUROZONE: The credit squeeze is easing.\par \par \uc2 --August M3 rose 2.0% on the year against a forecast rise of 1.9%.\par \par \uc2 --August private sector lending fell 1.5% from 1.6% in July.\par \par \uc2 Could it really be that the eurozone's credit squeeze is starting to ease? The pace at which private sector lending is falling has slowed, with lending to households picking up, even if business credit continues to contract. Meanwhile, the pace of money supply growth continues to pick up. Once the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent policy provisions kick in, they should boost credit and money supply further still. (AM)\par \par \uc2 CHINA: Chinese leaders are discussing replacing \uc2 Zhou Xiaochuan\uc2 , the governor of the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 , our Beijing bureau reported. The move reflects disagreements within the government over the financial reform agenda and concerns that Mr. Zhou's push for those reforms might stymie growth.\par \par \uc2 Domestic stock markets celebrated this news, the assumption being that it would usher in looser monetary policy, but if the change goes through, the long-term signal to foreign investors could be very bad indeed. Mr. Zhou has been the voice of reform, pushing for badly needed changes to the Chinese financial system--including interest rate liberalization and an open capital account. Without those reforms, it's hard to see how the Chinese economy can break free of an unsustainable model of growth in which artificially cheap bank loans finance relentless investment and construction without building up the consumer base. If Mr. Zhou's reform agenda is abandoned, it might ensure that there's more growth in the short term as that model is allowed to run a little longer, but the eventual downturn could be all the more painful when the investment bubble bursts. (MC)\par \par \uc2 TAIWAN: The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left its benchmark interest rates unchanged as expected, but signaled that tighter monetary policy could be on the horizon, with the governor, Perng Fai-nan, later saying that the monetary policy "tone" is switching to "neutral" from "accommodative."\par \par \uc2 Taiwanese rates haven't budged since July 2011, so the mere signal of a change is significant news. It reflects the surprising pickup that the local economy has undergone in recent months as demand for its all-important microchip and electronic components exports has surged. (MC)\par \par \uc2 AUSTRALIA: The \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2 's governor, \uc2 Glenn Stevens\uc2 , said so-called macroprudential tools could prove useful in dousing the hot housing market, although he retained some doubts about their effectiveness .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Stevens's comments follow an RBA report Wednesday that it is talking to \uc2 Australia Prudential Regulation Authority\uc2 , or APRA, about containing mortgage lending. The RBA is trying to walk a tightrope between cooling the housing market on the one hand and keeping liquidity supplied to the otherwise weak broader economy on the other. (MC)\par \par \uc2 TURKEY: The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Turkey\uc2  kept its one-week repo interest rate at 8.25% and all other benchmark rates unchanged.\par \par \uc2 The decision could be seen as something of a compromise for \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It is under pressure to cut rates from the government to boost jobs, with the unemployment rate up at an unhealthy 9.9%, but the economy is also facing inflation at 9.54%, well above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target of 5%, which would normally demand a rate hike. The decision reflects a political bind, in other words, perpetuating a difficult situation that will keep investors wary of buying the Turkish lira. (MC)\par \par \uc2 CZECH REPUBLIC: The \uc2 Czech National Bank\uc2  kept its benchmark rate at 0.05% and made no change to its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy to weaken the koruna.\par \par \uc2 With the ECB now adopting fresh stimulus and the euro falling again, it's little surprise the \uc2 Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  took the decision to continue with monetary accommodation and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 Czech Republic\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic circumstances are about as stark a contrast as you get from Turkey's. In fact, it's in a similar situation as Switzerland. The Czech economy has low inflation and is potentially at risk of deflation. Its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has set ultra-low interest rates but, like Switzerland's, has also been forced to adopt a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program to prevent the koruna from advancing above a fixed threshold euro. The Czech Republic has a healthy banking industry, also like Switzerland, but an export sector that's hurting from the eurozone's slowdown.\par \par \uc2 COMING UP:\par \par \uc2 U.S.: 8:30 a.m. EDT.\par \par \uc2 --August advancer report on durable goods. [Expected -17.5% vs. +22.6% in July.]\par \par \uc2 Don't be alarmed by those gigantic swings in the forecast. July's headline number was wildly distorted by some big aircraft sales for \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , and now economists expect payback in August. In fact, if you look at the ex-transportation number from last month's report it was down -0.8%, so that adjusted figure may even head in the other direction.\par \par \uc2 --Unemployment insurance weekly claims report. [Initial Claims expected 296,000 vs. 280,000 in prior week.]\par \par \uc2 After a couple of weeks in which the claims data had backed up a bit, we headed sharply lower again last week, re-affirming the impression that the labor market is truly now sustaining far fewer layoffs underway. This number at least--if not wages--reflects tighter labor conditions. It's one of those data points that helps build the hawk's case for a rate hike sooner rather than later.\par \par \uc2 U.S.: Time N/A. \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Atlanta\uc2 Dennis Lockhart\uc2 's speech at the U.S. Senator Thad Cochran Forum on American Enterprise.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lockhart is this year a non-voter on the FOMC but is seen as an important moderate voice on the committee. So comments of his recently in which he was clearly reluctant to declare victory in the U.S. economic recovery and thus to consider a rate hike soon in the New Year were an important indication of the barriers to monetary tightening within the FOMC.\par \par \uc2 JAPAN: 7:30 p.m. EDT. (8:30 a.m. Friday, Tokyo)\par \par \uc2 --August national consumer prices index. [Core \uc2 CPI\uc2  was +3.3% on-year, overall \uc2 CPI\uc2  was +3.4% on-year, unchanged on month.]\par \par \uc2 --September \uc2 CPI\uc2  for Tokyo. [Core \uc2 CPI\uc2  was +2.7% on-year, overall \uc2 CPI\uc2  was +2.8% on-year, +0.2% on month.]\par \par \uc2 In the national \uc2 CPI\uc2  data for Japan you need to look at the monthly data. The headline annual numbers are distorted by the impact of the one-off sales tax increase in April, but in the monthly numbers we see, once again, an inclination toward disinflation. The good news is that Tokyo's data look a bit more reasonable and these lead the rest of the nation, since they come out a month earlier. Still, if there is no clear sign of the return to inflation sought by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  may have to act. Markets will feel more convinced of that following comments from Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  last week, who vowed to do what's necessary to achieve the BOJ's 2% annual inflation target (free of sales-tax-hike effects.) \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 25, 2014 07:28 ET (11:28 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140925ea9p001ju\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
