\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-Central European factory activity slows, Poland nears stagnation\b0\par\par\uc2 768 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juli 2014\par 11:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Combines stories, adds quotes, details)\par \par \uc2 * Polish manufacturing PMI at 12-mth low of 50.3\par \par \uc2 * Czech PMI down to 54.7, below fcast, but still solid\par \par \uc2 * Data reflect divergence of Czech, Polish monetary policies\par \par \uc2 * Hungary's PMI falls to 51.5\par \par \uc2 By Marcin Goettig\par \par \uc2 WARSAW, July 1 (Reuters) - Factory activity slowed across central and eastern Europe in June, with Poland nearly in stagnation and the pace in the Czech Republic declining, although the latter is still growing and is cushioned by the country's ultra-loose monetary stance.\par \par \uc2 The manufacturing PMI index for Poland fell to a lower-than-expected 50.3 points last month, a 12-month low, from 50.8 in May, data compiled by Markit and \uc2 HSBC\uc2  showed.\par \par \uc2 Anything above 50 implies growth, but in this case very slight.\par \par \uc2 The data raised the pressure on the \uc2 Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ease its policy, economists said, as Poland has one of the highest real interest rates in the region and is facing the risk of deflation.\par \par \uc2 Poland's monetary stance is in contrast with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Czech Republic\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has slashed rates to almost zero and is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the crown.\par \par \uc2 The Czech Purchasing Managers' Index (PMI) dropped to 54.7 in June from a 3-year high hit last month. The slowdown followed a decline in PMI in Germany, the region's main trade partner.\par \par \uc2 But the Czech data was still firmly in growth territory, aided by a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy of weakening the crown \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has helped exporters.\par \par \uc2 The Polish PMI data came after a disappointing retail sales and industrial output reading last month, signalling that economic growth was likely to slow compared with the first quarter, when the economy expanded by 3.4 percent annually.\par \par \uc2 "June survey data from \uc2 HSBC\uc2  signalled a broad stagnation in Polish manufacturing business conditions," Markit said.\par \par \uc2 "As for the H2 2014/2015 outlook, improving labour market, strong investment performance in Q1 2014, recovering credit still suggest that the current weakness should not extend," said \uc2 HSBC\uc2  CEE economist Agata Urbanska-Giner.\par \par \uc2 Hungary, which uses a different methodology for its PMI index, reported the index dropped to 51.5 in June from a revised 53.8 in May and was below the average of the past three years.\par \par \uc2 CONTRAST\par \par \uc2 The decline in the Czech PMI was mostly driven by external factors, said \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's Urbanska-Giner.\par \par \uc2 "This correction is in line with the German PMI's slide from the beginning of the year," she said. "Industrial production growth is still likely to be climbing higher in the near term."\par \par \uc2 The Czech index remained in growth for the 14th successive month.\par \par \uc2 After exiting a record year-and-a-half recession last year, the export-reliant Czech economy's recovery has gathered pace in the past two quarters, lifted by better trade with euro zone partners and improving domestic demand.\par \par \uc2 Revised data from the statistics office on Tuesday showed the economy grew faster than previously thought in the first quarter, expanding by 0.8 percent on a quarterly basis and by 2.9 percent on an annual basis, the fast since early 2011.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the crown in November to fight deflation risks, helping boost exports and retail sales as shoppers stopped waiting for falling prices.\par \par \uc2 Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has, in contrast, been reluctant to cut rates, still remembering years of stubbornly high inflation and afraid that strong economic growth would at some point trigger a rise in consumer prices.\par \par \uc2 Inflation normally accelerates in tandem with economic growth, but in Poland that link has been broken because credit has been slow, the government is reducing public debt and low inflation in the euro zone, Poland's biggest trade partner, has had a knock-on effect.\par \par \uc2 Poland's inflation stands at 0.2 percent in annual terms, deep below the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  target of 2.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 "Our base-case scenario envisages a stabilisation of rates until year-end, but we note that the risk of a downward move in rates is increasing," Antoniak said, adding that the data also points to a slowdown in second-quarter GDP growth.\par \par \uc2 Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  starts its 2-day meeting on rates on Tuesday and will deliver its decision on Wednesday. All but one economists polled by Reuters last week expect the bank to keep its main rate at 2.5 percent. (Writing by Marcin Goettig; Additional reporting by Krisztina Than in BUDAPEST and Jason Hovet in PRAGUE Editing by Jeremy Gaunt) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-7-1T10:32:13.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140701ea710054y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
