\par\par \b \uc2 CEE Morning Briefing: Region Watching Fed, Polish Minutes Due\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sean Carney   \par \uc2 666 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2013\par 04:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Emerging Markets Report\par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par Local/GMT \par 1130/0930 Hungary government bond auction \par 1400/1200 Polish minutes from Sept rate meeting \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Central European markets Thursday morning will have their first chance to react to decision of the U.S. Federal Reserve's policy meeting, which surprised markets on Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Federal Open Market Committee said in its post-meeting policy-setting statement that it would continue to buy $85 billion in bonds each month. Investors had expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pare its bond-buying programs by between $10 billion and $15 billion at this meeting.\par \par \uc2 "It's amazing, and certainly not what we thought would happen," said \uc2 Marc Doss\uc2 , a San Diego, Calif.-based regional chief investment officer with Wells Fargo Private Bank. "We were definitely in the camp they would do $10 to $15 billion" less purchasing each month.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in the region, the \uc2 Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will publish minutes from its September monetary policy meeting when it left rates unchanged.\par \par \uc2 Poland's monetary policy council cut the benchmark rate to an all-time low of 2.5% in an easing campaign between November and July as inflation and growth slowed hand in hand. In August inflation reached 1.1% on year, significantly below the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2.5% goal.\par \par \uc2 Comments from monetary policy council members recently are centered on the message that interest rates will remain on hold at least until end-2013.\par \par \uc2 In Budapest, Hungary will offer a total of 44 billion forints ($197 million) worth of three-year, five-year and 15-year bonds at an auction Thursday. The series on offer are reopened issues.\par \par \uc2 The agency last auctioned the three- and five-year bonds on Sept. 5, while the 15-year bond was last reopened in April.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par EUR/CZK \par Latest 0150 GMT 25.731-72 \par Previous 2150 GMT 25.742-817 \par %Chg -0.11 \par  \par EUR/HUF \par Latest 0150 GMT 295.31-6.36 \par Previous 2150 GMT 25.742-817 \par % Chg +0.12 \par  \par EUR/PLN \par Latest 0150 GMT 4.1571-668 \par Previous 2150 GMT 4.1599-695 \par % Chg -0.07 \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par  \par Hungary \par         Wed    Tue \par 3 yrs   4.70%  4.76% \par 5 yrs   5.30%  5.40% \par 10yrs   5.89%  6.00% \par  \par Poland \par         Wed    Tue \par 3 yrs   3.31%  3.28%(1) \par 5 yrs   3.79%  3.80% \par 10yrs   4.42%  4.42% \par (1) 2 yrs \par  \par Czech Republic \par         Wed    Tue \par 3 yrs   0.37%  0.38% \par 5 yrs   1.27%  1.25% \par 10yrs   2.41%  2.40%(1) \par (1) 11 yrs \par  \par STOCKS \par  \par WIG 20 \par 2,363.78 -1.58 -0.07% \par BUX \par 18,454.66 -174.43 -0.94% \par PX \par 975.47 -2.43 -0.25% \par  \par  \par OTHER NEWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Industrial production in Poland slowed more than expected in August, but seasonally adjusted growth showed the country's economic recovery is continuing, official data showed Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Industrial output rose by an annual rate of 2.2% in August from 6.3% in July, Poland's statistics office said, slightly below the 2.4% forecast in a survey of 14 economists by The Wall Street Journal.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : Low inflation may force the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to weaken the koruna, the bank's governor said Wednesday, keeping alive a threat that has lost some of its conviction nearly a year after his first warning.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I still think that further policy relaxation is going to be needed, and for that we will have to provide the real thing," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Miroslav Singer\f28 \uc2 \f2  said when asked about the possibility of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in an interview published on the bank's website.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Veronika Gulyas in Budapest and Patryk Wasilewski in Warsaw contributed to this article.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Sean Carney at sean.carney@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Go to \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope/}}}\uc2 \f2  for the WSJ blog on Central and Eastern Europe, covering business, politics, society and more, written by our correspondents across the region. [ 19-09-13 0222GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091   \par \par \uc2 Dokument EM00000020130919e99j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
