\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Monetary Easing to Continue in Central Europe Next Week\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sean Carney and Patryk Wasilewski   \par \uc2 728 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juni 2013\par 12:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 PRAGUE--Further monetary easing is likely in Central Europe next week with an expected lowering of interest rates in Poland, following a cut in Hungary and dovish talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Czech Republic\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In Warsaw, \uc2 Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to cut its benchmark interest rates 0.25 percentage point to a new all-time low of 2.5%, all 18 analysts polled by Dow Jones Newswires said.\par \par \uc2 The aim is to help the slowing economy regain momentum and to bring slowing inflation closer to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target of 2.5%, which is the midpoint in the wider 1.5% to 3.5% tolerance range.\par \par \uc2 The rate council has lowered borrowing costs by two percentage points since November 2012 as the economy nearly stagnated in the first quarter with annual growth of 0.5%, while inflation eased to 0.5% in May.\par \par \uc2 Originally some market participants saw a risk of a deeper interest rate cut but the recently weakening zloty has shifted expectations.\par \par \uc2 Since the rate council's early June meeting the zloty has weakened from 4.25 against the euro to 4.32 midday Friday.\par \par \uc2 "If you asked me a few weeks ago I would have said there is a risk of a 0.5 percentage point rate cut," Raiffeisen Bank economist Marta Petka-Zagajewska said. "However, with the zloty where it is I see a risk there will be no cut at all."\par \par \uc2 The cut in Poland would follow a similar move in Hungary, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut the policy rate 25 basis points to 4.25% on June 25 amid an easing cycle that began in August 2012 in a bid to liven up its lackluster economy.\par \par \uc2 And would come as the \uc2 Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has its benchmark rate at the historic low of 0.05%, on Thursday said the likelihood of launching an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the koruna is increasing as it foresees "no tangible inflationary risks."\par \par \uc2 As it has no more room to lower interest rates, the next available tool is the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 "Risks to [the economy] are generally disinflationary; they are pointing towards the need for looser monetary conditions than is expected in [the bank's] forecasts," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said without adding when a move may come.\par \par \uc2 In Prague, households are slowing returning to shops and spending, but the annual increase in retail sales posted in April is likely to be offset by a minor decrease in May.\par \par \uc2 The data will follow the early-Friday release of revised Czech gross domestic product data that showed household consumption contracted by a mild 0.5% annually in the first quarter and it actually increased by 1.7% on a quarterly basis in the period.\par \par \uc2 Households are spending more in step with the government increasing its spending by 1.2% on year and 1% on a quarterly basis in the first three months of this year. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Date   GMT  Country Indicator    Period  Forecast    Previous \par Jul 01 0700  HUN    PMI          June    N/A         47.1** \par Jul 01 0700  POL    PMI          June    48.2(10)    48.0 \par Jul 01 0730  CZE    PMI          June    50.0(5)     50.1 \par Jul 01 1200  CZE  Budget Balance June    N/A        -CZK39.78B \par Jul 02 0930  HUN 3-Month T-Bill Tender   N/A         4.28%* \par Jul 03 N/A   POL  Interest rates July    2.5%(18) 