\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS- \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2 , stocks fall on Fed and economy worries\b0\par\par\uc2 638 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2013\par 09:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BUCHAREST, May 23 (Reuters) - Hungary's forint led emerging European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower on Thursday after hawkish comments by U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2  knocked riskier assets around the world.\par \par \uc2 Central European assets, offering better returns than more developed markets because of their higher degree of risk, have benefited from a wall of cheap money created by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stimulus. However, they often suffer more when global sentiment sours.\par \par \uc2 Stocks fell across the region, led by a 1.3 percent drop in Warsaw, after Bernanke's remarks sparked worries of an earlier than expected reduction in U.S. monetary stimulus, while weak Chinese economic data added to worries.\par \par \uc2 "The weakening is due to the Fed, what Bernanke said, which triggered a rise in risk aversion and we are affected the most," a Budapest dealer said. "Everything is weakening but we weaken more as the forint had firmed the most before."\par \par \uc2 The forint, which had gained 5 percent in the last month, fell 0.6 percent to 290.75 per euro by 0741 GMT, and was followed by Poland's zloty, which dropped 0.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 Expectations of lower interest rates are also keeping some pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to cut next week and more easing is also seen in Poland.\par \par \uc2 Rates are close to zero in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Czech Republic\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has threatened to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the crown to help a recession-hit economy. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was 0.1 percent higher on the day and analysts said pressure would persist given the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action.\par \par \uc2 In Romania, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also flagged a rate cutting cycle ahead, the leu lost 0.2 percent to 4.357 per euro, continuing a retreat from a 16-month high hit early in May.\par \par \uc2 The finance ministry may also struggle to sell 10-year debt at a tender later in the day, given yields on the secondary market rose about 15 basis points from Wednesday, and analysts and dealers said longer-term the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could continue to fall towards 4.40.\par \par \uc2 "The whole market was looking for a reason to sell, so the \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par region is under pressure," said a Bucharest dealer.     \par                    CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 0941 CET\par ************************** \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ************************\par                           Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                           bid     close      change  in 2013\par Czech crown                 26.064    26.086  +0.08%  -3.90%\par Hungarian forint           290.750   289.100   -0.57% +0.07%\par Polish zloty                 4.192     4.175    -0.39%  -2.80%\par Romanian leu                 4.357     4.347   -0.24%  +1.97%\par Croatian kuna                7.573     7.573    0.00%  -0.29%\par Serbian dinar              110.750   110.780   +0.03%  +1.40%\par Note: daily change calculated from previous close at 1600 GMT\par **************************** STOCKS **************************\par                           Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                                   close      change  in 2013\par Prague                      977.