\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: Hungary's Budgetary Outlook, EU Tax Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sean Carney   \par \uc2 1,063 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2013\par 04:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par Local/GMT \par 1030/0930 Czech central banker Hampl at conference on EU tax \par 1030/0930 Hungary PM speaks at Bruegel Institute \par 1130/1030 Hungary auctions 12-month T-bill \par 1545/1445 Hungary PM Orban, EC President Barroso briefing \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungary's budgetary outlook will be under scrutiny in Brussels on Wednesday, while a Czech central banker will speak on a \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  proposal to directly tax the bloc's citizens.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Hungarian Prime Minister Viktor Orban will meet European Commission President \uc2 Jose Manuel Barroso\uc2  in Brussels on Wednesday to talk about the country's budget and whether the EC will allow Hungary to exit the Excessive Deficit Procedure.\par \par \uc2 The EDP, which Hungary has been under since joining the \uc2 European Union\uc2  in 2004, acts as fiscal-restraint mechanism on countries that fail to reduce their budget deficits to below the EU's ceiling of 3% of gross domestic product.\par \par \uc2 As Hungary's economy has been shrinking due to falling investment and domestic consumption over the past two years, Mr. Orban's government has been reliant on widely criticized taxes on international companies that harm business confidence, growth and employment in the medium and longer term, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 The Commission said Monday that rather than seek revenues via controversial taxes, Mr. Orban's government should instead cut spending.\par \par \uc2 In Prague, Mojmir Hampl, the \uc2 Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's vice governor and an outspoken euro-skeptic, will speak on EU proposals for a pan-EU tax, that would likely be incorporated into existing value-added taxes, in theory giving the bloc its own revenue stream free of reliance on contributions from national governments.\par \par \uc2 The idea is part of a two-pronged attempt by Brussels to raise revenue for the bloc, the other element being a tax on financial transactions.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Hampl may also comment on the outlook for monetary easing in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Czech Republic\b0 \highlight\uc2  via \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  exhausted its standard monetary-policy toolbox last autumn when it cut its benchmark interest rate to 0.05%.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par EUR/CZK \par Latest 0250 GMT 25.605-77 \par Previous 2150 GMT 25.611-74 \par %Chg 0.00 \par  \par EUR/HUF \par Latest 0250 GMT 295.65-6.14 \par Previous 2150 GMT 295.35-6.39 \par % Chg +0.01 \par  \par EUR/PLN \par Latest 0250 GMT 4.1831-910 \par Previous 2150 GMT 4.1828-903 \par % Chg +0.01 \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par  \par Hungary \par         Tues   Mon \par 3 yrs   5.45%  5.48% \par 5 yrs   5.85%  5.95% \par 10yrs   6.40%  6.45% \par  \par Poland \par         Tues   Mon \par 3 yrs   3.19%  3.20(1) \par 5 yrs   3.39%  3.45% \par 10yrs   3.93%  3.98% \par (1) 2 yrs \par  \par Czech Republic \par         Tues   Mon \par 3 yrs   0.34%  0.35% \par 5 yrs   1.19%  1.19% \par 10yrs   1.92%  1.92% \par  \par STOCKS \par  \par WIG 20 \par 2,524.36 +2.86 +0.11% \par  \par BUX \par 19,506.04 +60.51 +0.31% \par  \par PX \par 1,019.97 +7.40 +0.73% \par  \par EU Enlarged \par 1,932.90 +10.35 +0.54% \par  \par OTHER NEWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Italian bank \f28 \uc2 \f2 UniCredit SpA\f28 \uc2 \f2  (UCG.MI) Tuesday launched the sale of up to 9.1% of its Polish subsidiary \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Pekao SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  to institutional investors through an accelerated bookbuilding.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bank said bookbuilding will start immediately and, at the end of it, it will continue to retain a majority shareholding in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Pekao\f28 \uc2 \f2  of at least 50.1%. The Italian bank also said it continues to be fully committed to Pekao, which remains core to its strategy, as well as Poland.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Polish economic growth slowed significantly last year, data showed on Tuesday, raising fears that the economy could soon be on the verge of contracting for the first time in more than two decades.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A downturn would be a tough blow for Poland, which until recently had stood up well against fallout from the debt crisis afflicting the neighboring euro zone, its largest trading partner.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC: Czech Prime Minister Petr Necas Tuesday dismissed renewed calls by his junior coalition partners to adopt the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 's fiscal compact and subject Czech budgetary policies to EU oversight.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Necas instead proposed that the Czech government draft its own fiscal responsibility rules, including setting limits on the level of the national debt. The new rules would require a constitutional amendment, Mr. Necas said in a statement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Hungary's Development Ministry is preparing further utility tariff cuts that would reduce water, sewage, communal waste collection, and chimney inspection fees, a government official told state news agency MTI Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government discussed the possibility of the price cuts at its Tuesday meeting as well as the results of the 10% price cut in retail electricity prices that came in to affect on Jan. 1, state secretary Janos Fonagy told the news agency.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Hungary's rate setters regard their current monetary policy tool set as adequate to maneuver, the rate setters said Tuesday in response to recent comments from the government that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should help it boost growth with unconventional monetary policy measures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Monetary Policy Council was fully unanimous with regard to this issue," Andras Simor, governor of the National Bank of Hungary, said at a press conference after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cut rates as expected by financial markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: Peruvian President Ollanta Humala met with his Romanian counterpart, Traian Basescu, on Tuesday to discuss several areas where the countries could increase cooperation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During a statement following the meeting, the presidents said they discussed increasing ties in the areas of education, health care, technology, and trade. "We have a lot of potential [to increase trade]," Mr. Humala said. "Our economies are complementary."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BULGARIA: A referendum in Bulgaria on whether to build a second nuclear power plant has failed due to insufficient turnout, official results showed Tuesday. Only 1.4 million people, or just over 20% of the electorate, voted in Sunday's poll, far short of the 60% needed for the plebiscite to be valid, electoral officials said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Margit Feher in Budapest contributed to this briefing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sean Carney\f28 \uc2 \f2  at sean.carney@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Go to \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope/}}}\uc2 \f2  for the WSJ blog on Central and Eastern Europe, covering business, politics, society and more, written by our correspondents across the region. [ 01-29-13 2216ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020130130e91u0000v\par }\page {\p