\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Czech Central Banker Lizal Sees No Inflationary Pressures\b0\par\par\uc2 888 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Dezember 2012\par 15:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 10 Dec 2012 09:58 EDT *DJ  Czech Lizal: Next Monetary Easing Likely Mid-2013\par \par \uc2 10 Dec 2012 09:58 EDT *DJ  Czech Lizal: \b \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Next Tool For Monetary Easing\par \par \uc2 10 Dec 2012 09:59 EDT *DJ  Czech Lizal: Near-Zero Rates Possible Into 2H 2014\par \par \uc2 10 Dec 2012 09:59 EDT *DJ  Czech Lizal: Quantitative Easing Makes No Sense for Czech Econ\par \par \uc2 10 Dec 2012 10:04 EDT DJ  Czech Central Banker Lizal Sees No Inflationary Pressures\par \par \uc2 By Sean Carney\par \par \uc2 PRAGUE--Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the koruna may come in mid-2013 and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's near-zero interest rates may remain until late 2014 as no inflationary pressures can be seen, a central banker said Monday.\par \par \uc2 In an interview, Lubomir Lizal, a member of the bank's monetary policy board, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's record-low benchmark interest rate of 0.05% will remain in place until inflationary pressures emerge.\par \par \uc2 When asked if he sees any inflationary risks at present, he simply said "no."\par \par \uc2 When asked how long rates can remain near zero, he said "I'd rather target conditions and not periods of time."\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's own forecasts, which span a period of 18 months, there are no indications of any rising pressures on consumer prices, he said.\par \par \uc2 The country's headline inflation rate was above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 1% to 3% target range in the first 10 months of the year, but that was largely due to increases in value-added taxes that took effect in January.\par \par \uc2 Monetary policy-relevant inflation, which looks at prices that can be influenced by monetary policy, has been in the lower end of the target inflation range all year, taking pressure off rate-setters.\par \par \uc2 The Czech economy has been in recession since late 2011, as domestic demand tumbled amid falling real wages, tax hike-fueled inflation, rising unemployment, government spending cuts and negative fallout from the euro zone's many crises.\par \par \uc2 As a result the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has largely exhausted its standard monetary toolbox centered on interest rates and now is considering how to further ease monetary conditions if the need should arise.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lizal said that now that interest rates are close to zero, "the natural question is what's next. For a small, open economy like the \b \highlight22\uc2 Czech Republic\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in current conditions \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is the best second tool you have."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't considering other non-standard monetary tools such as quantitative easing, as the Czech financial system already enjoys a surplus of liquidity and putting more money into the system wouldn't help.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lizal said he doesn't favor a particular \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate for the koruna, but rather will gauge policy needs via inflation, economic performance and current market developments.\par \par \uc2 "Based on the latest prognosis from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it suggests further monetary easing in the middle of next year," Mr. Lizal said, adding that the bank's forecasts have been largely accurate in the recent past and so he is comfortable with this estimation.\par \par \uc2 The discussion now is about the tool to use, not how and when to use it, he said without directly responding to a question on what \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate he'd like to see.\par \par \uc2 On Oct. 19, Kamil Janacek, Mr. Lizal's peer on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's seven-strong monetary policy board, said an exchange rate of above 25 koruna ($0.11) to the euro is good for exporters and the economy and is the strongest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate he'd like to see so long as the economy is weak.\par \par \uc2 At 1402 GMT Monday, the koruna traded at 25.25 to the euro. The koruna has remained above 25 to the euro since late October.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lizal also said any recovery by the Czech export-based economy hinges on a return to growth in the euro zone, which is the Czech Republic's largest trading partner.\par \par \uc2 Write to Sean Carney at sean.carney@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 Go to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope/ }}}\uc2  for the new Dow Jones blog on Central and Eastern Europe, covering business, politics, society and more, written by our correspondents across the region.\par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 This item was corrected at 22:21 GMT to fix the misstated impact of a potential quantitative easing in the 11th paragraph as saying it could fuel inflation. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't considering other non-standard monetary tools such as quantitative easing, as the Czech financial system already enjoys a surplus of liquidity and putting more money into the system wouldn't help.\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires }}}\par \par \uc2 10 Dec 2012 12:32 EDT DJ  Correction to Czech Central Banker Lizal story\par \par \uc2 "Czech Central Banker Lizal Sees No Inflationary Pressures," at 1504 GMT, misstated the impact of a potential quantitative easing in the 11th paragraph as saying it could fuel inflation. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't considering other non-standard monetary tools such as quantitative easing, as the Czech financial system already enjoys a surplus of liquidity and putting more money into the system wouldn't help.\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires }}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 10, 2012 12:32 ET (17:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020121210e8ca003u0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
