\par\par \b \uc2 Flight From Risk Thrashes Emerging-Market  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2 , Debt\b0\par\par\uc2 711 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2011\par 22:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -Global economic anxieties pummel emerging-market assets\par \par \uc2 -Host of central banks suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  as their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumble\par \par \uc2 -South Africa, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Czech Republic\b0 \highlight\uc2  hold policy rates steady\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Cynthia Lin \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Emerging-market assets fell under severe pressure Thursday as fears about the euro-zone credit crisis and global economic slowdown hit a boiling point despite the Federal Reserve's latest stimulus plan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. central bankers confirmed market expectations Wednesday, announcing that it will implement what most market participants call "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Twist," by buying longer-dated Treasurys and selling shorter-dated ones. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's decision did little to allay mounting fears about the sputtering global economy, sparking an exodus out of risky assets and into the refuge of U.S. government debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As Treasury yields plummeted, the risk premium investors demand to hold what they perceive as riskier emerging-market debt increased. This spread as reflected on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  Emerging Markets Bond Index Global, or Embig, was 32 basis points wider to 449 basis points above Treasurys. In price terms, the index lost 1.49%. The Latin America regional sub-index lost 1.69% in price as its spread blew out 34 basis points to 5.14% over Treasurys.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Emerging-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also took a beating across the board as the U.S. dollar soared, led by notable declines in the Mexican peso, Chilean peso and Korean won.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A slew central banks in Asia--including Korea, Taiwan and Thailand--were suspected to have \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to lend support to their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The Korean won was a notable underperformer in its region, weakening to KRW1,181.1 a dollar from KRW1,148.6--now the weakest since last September, according to CQG.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Brazil, the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  announced midday and quickly implemented an auction of $2.7 billion in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swap contracts to rein in the depreciating real. The battered Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  opened at 1.92 reais to the dollar, weakening sharply to BRL1.94 at one point. On the Embig, Brazil's risk premium increased 29 basis points to 285 basis points, losing 0.97% in price.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Such efforts come in stark contrast to moves these central banks conducted just months ago, when many complained that their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were strengthening too quickly and hurting exporters at home. Earlier in the year, these assets showed impressive resilience even when the situation in advanced nations began to crumble. But the rout since the start of September shows that these regions are still inextricably linked to the developed world.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The emerging-market space is interesting because it held and held and held," said Michael Mata, portfolio manager at ING's global bond fund, "but, in the end, there's no way to decouple from 50% of global GDP." Mata's team believes there's still more room from emerging-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to fall as these nations' policymakers cut rates and reduce the interest-rate differential that made their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so appealing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These days, inflation and other issues that come with red-hot economic growth is taking a back seat, with fears of a double-dip recession and the intensifying euro-zone credit crisis dominating investors' fears. The latest economic reports Thursday show the euro zone's composite purchase manufacturing index falling into the sub-50 contraction territory for the first time since 2009, while China's PMI sank deeper in the contraction range to 49.4 from 49.9.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Africa's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  decided to keep its policy rate at 5.5%, caught in a dilemma of slowing growth and rising inflation. The South African rand weakened to ZAR8.2963 a dollar from ZAR8.2530, which Reserve Bank Governor Gill Marcus acknowledged is putting the country's goal of keeping inflation under 6% at risk.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Monetary authorities of the Czech Republic also convened Thursday and decided to keep rates steady a record-low 0.75%. Central and Eastern European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have been rattled this month give their ties to the neighboring euro zone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Cynthia Lin; Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-4403; cynthia.lin@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Tom Murphy contributed to this article. [ 22-09-11 2033GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110922e79m001bq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
