\par\par \b \uc2 DJ FOCUS: Czech Koruna Rally Still Has Further To Run\b0\par\par\uc2 839 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Februar 2011\par 15:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- Czech koruna rally expected to continue in the short term\par \par \uc2 -- Strong fundamentals and prospects for rate hikes offer support\par \par \uc2 -- In longer term, koruna strength could pose problems \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Clare Connaghan \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The Czech koruna has always been considered one of the safer \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bets in Eastern Europe, but new signs of coming interest-rate increases are bolstering its role as a regional favorite.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  surged to its highest level against the euro since October 2008 last week after the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board voted four-three to leave its interest rates unchanged. The surprisingly thin majority alongside the more positive tone in the post-meeting press conference has raised expectations among market participants that the Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could raise interest rates as early as next month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The next rate-setting meeting, scheduled for March 24, will also be the first one attended by economist Lubomir Lizal, a new appointee to the seven-member policy board whose policy-making preferences are virtually unknown.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The country's strong economic fundamentals, particularly compared with regional peers such as Hungary, also offer a positive backdrop for the koruna. For the short term at least, this rally looks like it has further to run, market participants say.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "All in all the outlook for the koruna remains extremely positive," said emerging-markets strategists at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a note to clients. The Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is likely to remain close to record highs against the euro "for the time being," the bank said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Czech Republic--a key exporter to the euro area--has long been a sweet spot for investors in Central and Eastern Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think it probably has the best fundamentals in the region," said Neil Shearing, a senior emerging-markets economist at Capital Economics in London, noting the Czech Republic's inflation outlook and current-account and fiscal position.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Against this backdrop, since the start of the year, the koruna has notched up hefty gains against the euro, climbing by more than 4%. That momentum picked up after Thursday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  meeting with the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pushing below the key CZK24 level against the koruna for the first time since October 2008.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  keeps pushing higher, it could start to raise concerns at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , forcing it to follow a course set out by a number of countries across Asia and Latin America, which have actively sold their local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in an effort to push them down and protect exports.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strength forms a headache for export-dependent nations such as the Czech Republic, because it makes the country's goods and services appear more expensive abroad.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risks are probably the highest in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Czech Republic\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Central and Eastern Europe," said Benoit Anne, head of emerging-market strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For now at least, the authorities haven't expressed too much concern about the koruna's strength.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The fact that there was no negative sound bites condemning appreciation means that the monetary policy council are still quite comfortable with the appreciation trend of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and it's kind of opening the door to further strength," said Elisabeth Gruie, an emerging-markets strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, some observers are doubtful this rally in the koruna can last.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We're really losing momentum in my view at these levels, particularly from a valuation perspective," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Anne.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also, interest rates in the Czech Republic are at a record low of 0.75%, which is substantially lower than neighboring Hungary, where rates are at 6.00%, and Poland, whose policy rates currently stand at 3.75%. Typically, investors prefer \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  linked with higher interest rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "On a long-term basis we're more cautious about the koruna," said Nigel Rendell, senior emerging-market strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 RBC Capital Markets\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "With normalization of interest rates globally and the U.S. and the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at some point expected to raise interest rates in the future, I'm not sure the Czech koruna will be the best performer in the region," he said, adding that he prefers the Polish zloty over the long term.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But even if the rally does fade, depreciation pressure on the koruna is unlikely to emerge, particularly given its safe-haven status in the region, market participants say.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There is very little leverage or volatility [in the koruna market] so it's kind of a safe bet," said BNP's Gruie.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RBC's Rendell agreed. "In times of uncertainty the Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  seems to do reasonably well," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 1335 GMT, the koruna was trading at CZK24.065 against the euro, compared with CZK23.995 late in New York Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Clare Connaghan, Dow Jones Newswires; +44 (0) 20 7842 9496, clare.connaghan@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Leos Rousek in Prague contributed to this article.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 07, 2011 09:02 ET (14:02 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110207e72700384\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
