\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING:  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Seen Weak, Equities Mixed\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sean Carney   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 1,504 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2010\par 04:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TODAY'S CALENDAR \par  \par    Local/GMT \par  \par    1000/0700 Meeting of Russia's State Duma \par    1800/1700 Poland's software company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Asseco Poland SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  releases 2009, 4Q earnings \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trading in equities and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in central Europe Friday may be volatile despite a lackluster schedule of events.\f28 \par \par \uc2 A rally over the last two weeks has brought new six-month highs to Central Europe's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but analysts said the trend may run out of steam as investors take profits.\par \par \uc2 What's more, in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Czech Republic\b0 \highlight\uc2  the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could verbally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in coming days if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rally continues as it would threaten the country's export competitiveness, bank \uc2 Ceska Sporitelna\uc2  said in a note.\par \par \uc2 In equity markets, Czech power company \uc2 CEZ AS\uc2  (BAACEZ.PR) may see volatile trade Friday due to the news late-Thursday that a sought after upgrade of its largest coal-fired power plant still faces challenges.\par \par \uc2 Czech Environment Minister Jan Dusik Thursday resigned after a disagreement with the prime minister over the issuance of environmental clearance for the renovation of CEZ's Prunerov power plant.\par \par \uc2 Dusik said he was unable to make the final ruling on the global environmental impact of the proposed revamp of the Prunerov power plant revamp as sought by CEZ while the Prime Minister wanted a final verdict.\par \par \uc2 In Warsaw, leading software integrator Assseco Poland SA (ACP.WA) is expected to report a slightly weaker set of numbers due to slower corporate demand and shrinking margins. The market will look for 2010 outlook, and in particular for signs of a revival in public sector demand.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par         Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                    2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par EUR/CZK 25.3900-10 25.3720-20 +0.07  25.3980 25.3950  -3.79 \par EUR/HUF 262.13-64  261.98-49  +0.06  262.31  262.37   -3.12 \par EUR/PLN 3.8775-25  3.8782-02  -0.02  3.8777  3.8784   -5.55 \par USD/RUB 29.285-90  29.302-07  -0.06  29.319  29.287   -3.39 \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Hungarian forint is seen hovering around current strong levels on favorable investor sentiment, a trader said, adding that the euro will likely trade between HUF262-HUF263.2 Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After a recent rally, the Polish zloty may be set for a corrective move Friday, with EUR/PLN bouncing toward the 3.8850 level, a local dealer said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Russian ruble, also having rallied, is likely to follow oil lower on profit-taking and a possible dip in oil prices, said a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trader at Uralsib brokerage. "This will be a very short-term move lower, and not a violation of the current trend. I would continue to buy the ruble for the medium term."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Czech koruna may still have room to post more gains, but after rallying to a six-month high this week, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is likely to loose momentum ahead of Czech elections, scheduled for May, said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ceska Sporitelna\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par  \par HUNGARIAN BOND YIELDS--The three-year benchmark bond yield was 6.08% late Thursday from 6.13% Wednesday afternoon. The five-year was 6.38% from 6.41%, and the 10-year was 7.05% from 7.15%. \par  \par POLISH BOND YIELDS--The average yield on two-year paper was 4.65% compared with 4.67% late Wednesday, on five-year at 5.17% compared with 5.19%, on 10-year at 5.54% compared with 5.55%. \par  \par CZECH BOND YIELD--The yield on the benchmark 10-year paper was again unchanged on the day at 4.13% late Thursday. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Polish bonds may weaken slightly on Friday, mainly because the recent rally made them ripe for a correction, a local trader said. "But if so, I don't expect any major movements," he added. "Average yields shouldn't rise higher than by five basis points."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The current strengthening trend on the Hungarian treasury bond market will likely sustain, a trader said. The market is pricing a 5% key rate level, which indicates that there's room for further rate cuts after the elections, he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCKS \par  \par WIG20                    2445.40   loss   20.6    dn 0.8% \par BUX                     24000.01   gain  219.3    up 0.9% \par PX                       1208.5    loss    9.6    dn 0.8% \par RTS                      1558.8    loss    4.7    dn 0.3% \par MICEX                    1435.45   loss    1.9    dn 0.13% \par DJ Stoxx EU Enlarged 15  2632.1    loss    9.4    dn 0.4% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN WARSAW: The WIG20 is likely to face resistance to gains amid "a flat correction," a local broker said, listing lack of impulses as the main reason for a massive selloff. "If 2400 support is defended, then the index is likely to move toward 2500 level again," he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN PRAGUE: Trades in Czech stocks are likely to remain lackluster Friday. Unless the U.S. markets post some recovery, Czech blue-chips will probably keep heading lower, a local trader said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN BUDAPEST: Hungarian stocks could take a breather and remain stable Friday after trading up Thursday afternoon, an Erste Bank trader said. The market mood will nevertheless probably remain positive, which the trader expects to help keep share prices at current high levels.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN MOSCOW: Russian indices may fall up to 1.5% on profit-taking Friday, said Andrei Kuk, a senior trader at Uralsib. "Equities have had quite a run in the last few days, and traders are looking to lock in profits. Oil turning lower gives them an opportunity to do this." [ 03-18-10 2358ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  OTHER NEWS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: Australia will proceed with its plans to allow the sale of uranium to Russia for use in its civil nuclear reactors but still won't allow sales of the fuel to India as it hasn't signed the Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty, Trade Minister Simon Crean said Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: Russian oil producer OAO Lukoil Holdings (LKOH.RS) agreed last year to borrow more than $1 billion from state-controlled lender OAO \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sberbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  (SBER.RS), nearly half at double-digit interest, business daily Vedomosti reports Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Coca-Cola Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (KO) intends to buy 100% of Russian fruit-juice maker Nidan Soki in a deal involving the company's founders and biggest shareholder, business daily Vedomosti reports Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: Prime Minister Vladimir Putin announced Thursday that Russia will commission Iran's first nuclear power plant in the port city of Bushehr this summer, prompting a swift rebuke from visiting U.S. Secretary of State Hillary Clinton.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: Canada's foreign minister Thursday dismissed Russian President Dmitry Medvedev's claim that fellow Arctic nations were sidelining Moscow in a race to tap the region's resources, as "playing games."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: The trade union at a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ford Motor Company\f28 \uc2 \f2  plant and the company's administration failed to agree on a pay rise and the improvement of working conditions, plant's trade union leader Alexei Etmanov told Interfax.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Polish Monetary Policy Council members were divided over the need to start policy tightening this year, official minutes of last month's policy meeting showed Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC: Czech Environment Minister Jan Dusik said Thursday he was resigning over differences with the prime minister on the revamp of a coal-fired power plant listed as one of the biggest polluters in Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC: Czech Environment Minister Jan Dusik Thursday tendered his resignation due to a disagreement with the prime minister over the issuance of environmental clearance for a planned renovation of a Czech coal-fired power plant owned by \f28 \uc2 \f2 CEZ AS\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BAACEZ.PR).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SLOVAKIA: South Korean car maker \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kia Motors Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (000270.SE) said Thursday it would build a new engine production facility in Slovakia, valued at EUR100 million.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Hungary's main opposition party, Fidesz, looks set to win next month's general election, but the chances of it securing the two-thirds majority it wants are fading, opinion polls showed Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Hungary's Government Debt Management Agency, or AKK, will offer a floating rate bond at an auction March 25, the first time it has issued this type of bond since June 2001.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: Romania's public debt fell to 143.95 billion lei ($48.3 billion) at the end of January from RON148.05 billion a month earlier, according to Finance Ministry data released Thursday, news agency Mediafax reports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: The Romanian economy has already hit bottom and it now faces the last phase of the crisis, with "huge" difficulties concerning the public sector budget, an official of the European Investment Bank said Thursday, news agency Mediafax reports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BULGARIA: Bulgaria and Turkey pledged to act jointly in energy projects such as the Nabucco gas pipeline, during Turkish Foreign Minister Ahmet Davutoglua's two-day visit to Sofia starting on Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Sean Carney, Dow Jones Newswires; +420 222 315 290;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 sean.carney@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Veronika Gulyas in Budapest, Malgorzata Halaba in Warsaw and Ira Iosebashvili in Moscow contributed to this briefing.) [ 03-18-10 2358ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100319e63j0002m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
