\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-E.Europe wins respite, rouble sinks\b0\par\par\uc2 707 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Februar 2009\par 13:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Central, Eastern European \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilise after falls\par \par \uc2 * Falling oil pressure as Russia's rouble, stocks fall\par \par \uc2 * Emerging stock markets lower on global worries\par \par \uc2 * Worries persist over Dubai state firm refinancing\par \par \uc2 By Peter Apps and Martina Fuchs\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Feb 18 (Reuters) - Central and Eastern European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  saw some respite on Wednesday after hitting multi-year lows the previous day, but weaker oil put Russia's rouble under renewed pressure and global emerging stocks sank.\par \par \uc2 Ongoing concern over contagion from emerging Europe into Western banks continued to undermine the euro, while in the Middle East concerns remained high Dubai might struggle to refinance loans for state companies.\par \par \uc2 Having dropped across the board on Tuesday undermined by worries from rating agencies and worsening economic outlooks, emerging European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed in morning trade.\par \par \uc2 The Polish zloty was up 1.39 percent by 1230 GMT, now down 15 percent since the start of the year and bouncing off record lows against the euro, and the Czech crown also rose 1.63 percent supported by rate hike expectations.\par \par \uc2 The Hungarian forint was up 0.97 percent off its own all-time lows, but still down 13.65 percent on the year and with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  warning it might take unspecified "unconventional means" to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The general picture is that there is some consolidation from yesterday," said \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  analyst Tim Ash.\par \par \uc2 But he said he he saw potential drastic policy responses across the region, which was until recently seen as one of the most attractive emerging markets but is now regarded amongst the most exposed to the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 "There is now talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Czech Republic\b0 \highlight\uc2  (and) of rate hikes, and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Poland on the back of dismal economic growth," he said.\par \par \uc2 Worries over growing defaults on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans in central and eastern Europe and the knock-on effect on local and Western parent banks helped push the euro to a 10 month low, a sign emerging Europe's woes might affect the whole continent.\par \par \uc2 Benchmark emerging sovereign debt spreads narrowed by one basis point to 692 oh the US Treasuries, a sign of slightly heightened appetite for emerging debt.\par \par \uc2 FALLING OIL HITS RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 But benchmark emerging equities lost 0.75 percent, now down 8.83 percent on the year with investors worried by global bank problems, plunging economic output and little sign of recovery despite the signing of a $787 billion US stimulus package into law.\par \par \uc2 Stocks in the Czech Republic and Russia were among the biggest losers, down 5.85 percent and 6.51 percent.\par \par \uc2 Falling oil prices renewed pressure on the Russian rouble, which fell 0.21 percent to reach 40.63 against its euro-dollar basket, again nearing the bottom of the basket at 41.\par \par \uc2 Having called a halt to a series of many devaluations last month by widening the basket sharply, Russia has vowed to defend the lower level but the market has repeatedly tried to test it.\par \par \uc2 "The support has been knocked out from under the market," said Andrei Kukk, dealer at Uralsib.\par \par \uc2 The cost of insuring Russia's debt against default in the credit default swaps market also increased slightly to 772 basis points from 769 on Tuesday, meaning it would cost $772,000 a year to cover $10 million of five-year debt.\par \par \uc2 The cost of insuring Dubai's debt against default was slightly lower at 940 basis points from Tuesday's 977 having topped 1000 late last week.\par \par \uc2 Magazine MEED reported on Tuesday state-owned Bourse Dubai would get a $1 billion bailout from the United Arab Emirates federal government to avoid default on a $3.4 billion loan.\par \par \uc2 While United Arab Emirates has benefited from colossal oil revenues in recent years, only 3-4 percent of Dubai's GDP comes from oil and it has racked up debt to fund expansion.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts believe Dubai might be forced to turn to oil-rich Abu Dhabi for support.\par \par \uc2 Deutche Bank estimates state owned companies in Dubai will need to refinance some $18 billion in obligations this year including the Bourse Dubai loan. (Editing by Andy Bruce)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-EMERGING|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090221e52i000ad\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
