\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Skoda Auto fears risks from strong Czech crown \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christiaan Hetzner \par \uc2 695 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2007\par 10:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited \par \par \uc2 FRANKFURT, March 28 (Reuters) - The unnatural strength of the Czech crown may force local industry to build up production abroad, if the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Czech Republic\b0 \highlight\uc2 's largest exporter told Reuters. \par \par \uc2 "The value of the crown <0#EURCZK=> does not reflect the strength of the country, which simply says that the crown is overvalued. The restructuring process is still going on in the country ... There are obviously speculative elements," Detlef Wittig, chairman of Volkswagen's Czech unit Skoda Auto, said in a recent interview. \par \par \uc2 Even though every second car sold in the Czech market is a Skoda, the country's largest company by revenue is still heavily dependent on foreign demand, with nearly 90 percent of its models delivered to customers abroad. \par \par \uc2 Wittig said the recovery of the Czech economy was due in large part to a buoyant domestic manufacturing industry, so the crown's strength was a risk to further growth. \par \par \uc2 When asked whether the Czech National Bank might act to help bring down the value of the crown, the Skoda boss replied: "The more often and louder we say so, maybe." \par \par \uc2 At 2.5 percent, the country's benchmark two-week repo rate is already the lowest in the European Union, and a Reuters poll showed that all 18 analysts surveyed expect rates to remain flat when the bank holds its monthly policy meeting on Thursday. \par \par \uc2 Skoda hopes to tap into dynamic demand from emerging markets by exporting kits to be assembled in destination countries such as Russia and India to boost sales to a record 600,000 cars this year, but its cost advantage over rivals is eroding as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciates. \par \par \uc2 "I feel very strongly about this issue. I don't expect that we will continue to supply all these foreign plants from the Czech Republic with a permanently stronger crown," the Skoda boss said. \par \par \uc2 "The question of faster, earlier localisation comes up." \par \par \uc2 GREEN CARS TO COME \par \par \uc2 Apart from its plant in Aurangabad, India, it already assembles cars from kits in Bosnia, Ukraine and Kazakhstan. \par \par \uc2 Skoda also plans to start manufacturing second-generation Octavias in China by the middle of this year, with annual production reaching 100,000 cars after it adds second-generation Fabia and Superb models in the coming years. \par \par \uc2 A further factor expected to exert upward pressure on costs are wage negotiations with its labour force, which Wittig said could be concluded as early as Friday. \par \par \uc2 "It is a major discussion and it's part of these long-term decisions that we will have to make," he said. \par \par \uc2 Skoda, which built its first car over a 100 years ago, has become a huge success story for Volkswagen ever since Europe's largest carmaker acquired it in April 1991. \par \par \uc2 Gradually shedding its poor image as the local carmaker of a formally communist country, production at Skoda has tripled, the model range has been overhauled and expanded, and both sales and profits are at record levels. \par \par \uc2 It expects to sell 600,000 units in 2007 -- a year earlier than planned -- a forecast that Wittig admitted was "on the safe side" thanks to the rollout of the new Fabia subcompact, which contributes just under half of the brand's sales volume. \par \par \uc2 "Skoda fits the present trend in the market, which is to buy smart -- to get all the technology that is needed with high quality, reliable cars at very affordable prices," he said. \par \par \uc2 Amid the ongoing discussion of reducing carbon dioxide emissions (CO2), Wittig said the brand was working on a similar concept to VW's Blue Motion range of fuel-saving cars, which he dubbed the Skoda Green Line, though he admitted it was unclear what kind of market potential these might have. \par \par \uc2 "It's difficult to say because we've seen this as a hype two or three times, and when we were ready with certain technical developments, the hype was over already ... This (CO2 discussion) may blow over and become more rational," he said.  \par \par \uc2 VOLKSWAGEN-SKODA/ (INTERVIEW)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|CZS|M|D \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070328e33s000la\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
