\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Czech rate cut does nothing to scare crown bulls.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Marek Petrus  \par \uc2 792 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2005\par 09:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 PRAGUE, Feb 3 (Reuters) - A surprise interest rate cut has failed to knock the Czech crown off of its bullish footing, and an outlook for solid economic growth and robust exports will keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  buoyant this year, analysts said on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Last week's 25 basis point cut in the Czech National Bank's policy rate to 2.25 percent did nothing to dent the market mood and inspired merely a knee-jerk fall which the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fully reversed within two sessions.  \par \par \uc2 It firmed to a near 2-1/2-year high of 29.950 per euro on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts at a number of major international investment banks believe the crown, which has firmed by 11 percent against its reference euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past year, has further to go as it catches up with last year's big rally in the Polish zloty.  \par \par \uc2 "We believe the CNB cut was not specifically meant to weaken the crown, suggesting \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk is limited," said Martin Blum, head of emerging markets research at HVB Group.  \par \par \uc2 Following the cut, Governor Zdenek Tuma played down concerns that the crown rise was hurting the economy, and stressed the bank's concern about undershooting its 3 percent inflation goal.  \par \par \uc2 "This, coupled with the firm balance of payments backdrop, means that we continue to target a steady grind lower to 29.90 per euro in the first quarter," Blum wrote in his research, and many others share his view.  \par \par \uc2 The cocktail of a shrinking and possibly even disappearing trade deficit and a healthy, nearly four percent economic expansion - outpacing flagging euro zone growth rates - makes the crown almost a one-way bet, despite minute yield premiums.  \par \par \uc2 Morgan Stanley predicts a rise to 29.50 per euro by the end of 2005, which its analyst Oliver Weeks thinks could provide impetus for another 25 basis point rate cut, a prospect that has buoyed the local debt market.  \par \par \uc2 CSFB forecasts a crown jump to 29.10 per euro within six months, near an all-time high of 28.90 reached in mid-2002.  \par \par \uc2 Citibank is even more bullish, looking for a firming to 29.50 within three months and 28.50 a year from now.  \par \par \uc2 Few have come out with a contrarian view, such as BNP Paribas which expects a steady drop towards 31.1 by end-2005.  \par \par \uc2 NEGLIGIBLE \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  RISK  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Czech Republic\b0 \highlight\uc2  boasts the lowest inflation and interest rates in central Europe, with last week's rate cut halving the premium over the short-term euro zone assets to a mere quarter of a percent, but the rate gap is not what drives the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "Even though the crown is not supported by yield pick-up strategies, its neighbouring \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are staying strong and this is in turn providing a positive background for the Czech crown," said Koon Chow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at CSFB in London.  \par \par \uc2 The Slovak crown has been the region's star performer this year, rallying to all-time highs against the euro almost daily.  \par \par \uc2 Rapid export growth, fed by a new Toyota-Peugeot-Citroen car plant that starts output this month, is forecast by some to cut the Czech current account gap towards 4 percent in 2005 from last year's more than five percent.  \par \par \uc2 Some analysts say the trade deficit, a traditional feature of post-Communist transformation, may disappear by 2006 when the plant will be working full out, producing 300,000 cars a year.  \par \par \uc2 They are also betting that at least part of inflows related to the upcoming sale of phone group Cesky Telecom (SPTTsp.PR) and other privatisations will pass through the market, even though the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to keep most of the funds at bay.  \par \par \uc2 The prospect of having to buy the nearly $3 billion proceeds from the Cesky Telecom sale is dampening \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appetite for market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as opposed to the Slovak bank which has repeatedly jumped into the fray to stem the rise of its crown.  \par \par \uc2 "While the crown offers the lowest yields in central Europe, the risks of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in other countries of the region, particularly in Hungary and Slovakia, may counterbalance this disadvantage from an investor point of view," said Radek Maly, head of research at Citibank in Prague.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bloated \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, at 20.9 billion euros at end-2004, equal almost five months of average imports, which economists consider very generous cover.  \par \par \uc2 Vice-Governor Ludek Niedermayer put it bluntly last week:  \par \par \uc2 "In my opinion, what we have seen so far, this is not a situation when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or when I personally, would think about \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ... It is a very exceptional tool."   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050203e123007cb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
