\par\par \b \uc2  East European  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Helped By Nice Treaty Yes Vote \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tyler Lifton \par \uc2 326 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 2002\par 17:31\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Eastern European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are broadly rallying Monday against the euro following Sunday's vote in Ireland to expand the European Union. \par \par \uc2  Voters in Ireland approved the Nice Treaty 63% to 47%, paving the way for the E.U. to be expanded by 10 countries, mostly eastern and central European, to 25 by 2004. \par \par \uc2  "There's been a 1.5%-2% move across the board stronger in Eastern European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 " against the euro since last  Friday, as polls were showing that Ireland would vote to expand the E.U., said Thomas Browne, emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist of Deutsche Bank. \par \par \uc2  Monday morning in New York, the euro was buying 241.95 Hungarian forint, 4.04 Polish zloty, and 30.54 Czech koruna. \par \par \uc2  The koruna stalled against the euro Monday, however, Browne said. While it gained some speed last week, the potential for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Czech Republic\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which it frequently does to keep the koruna from strengthening too much, is capping its gains. \par \par \uc2  One London-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader said that banks were buying Eastern European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in anticipation of "real  money" investors coming into the market in the coming days, such as fixed-income mutual funds investing in those countries. \par \par \uc2  Wike Groenenberg, director of Eastern European economics  at Citigroup in London, said that accession to the E.U. will help lower inflation and interest rates in the new member countries. \par \par \uc2  In addition, "More investors will feel comfortable investing in these countries and that will pave the way for" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation, Groenenberg said. \par \par \uc2  She believes that while the near-term upside for Eastern  European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have already been seen, they will likely further appreciate in the coming months as various other issues still pending regarding E.U. expansion are cleared. \par \par \uc2  -By Tyler Lifton, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2248 \par \par \uc2  -tyler.lifton@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020021021dyal005pl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
