\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Managed  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  regimes may make sense for EU hopefuls. \b0\par\par\uc2  By James Thornhill \par \uc2 846 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2002\par 16:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 6 (Reuters) - Managed \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes may not exactly fit the model of a modern liberalised economy, but some analysts think they may be appropriate for emerging European countries as they prepare for EU and euro entry. The argument is that a more closely managed system can help reduce the potential for volatility and cope better with the tendency of the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to move higher on strong investment inflows in the run up to European Union accession. \par \par \uc2  Upward pressure on the Czech crown, Hungarian forint and Polish zloty this year has led to mounting concern among politicians in those countries that competitiveness is being damaged. \par \par \uc2  "Active exchange rate management in some form is a logical response to the pressures leading to excessive appreciation ahead of EU membership," said Erik Nielsen, head of new European markets research at Goldman Sachs. \par \par \uc2  "Given continued strong FDI (foreign direct investment) and portfolio flows, a pure free-float could lead to overvaluation, increased volatility and ultimately threaten competitiveness and real convergence," he added. \par \par \uc2  Responses to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength this year have varied between countries, and now only Poland is running a complete free float. \par \par \uc2  In the \b \highlight22\uc2 Czech Republic\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell crowns on several occasions this year and lowered interest rates with explicit reference to excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength. \par \par \uc2  In Hungary, the new government pledged to combat forint strength as part of its election platform, while the Polish government is seeking an arrangement with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the zloty. \par \par \uc2  REGIME SHIFT IN POLAND? \par \par \uc2  The Czech Republic and Hungary already operate systems that place limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility. In Hungary's case, forint fluctuations are limited to 15 percent on either side of a central parity rate against the euro. \par \par \uc2  These trading bands replicate the Exchange Rate Mechanism 2 (ERM2) system that each country must join for two years prior to euro zone membership, as laid out in the Maastricht treaty. \par \par \uc2  In this sense, a managed \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  system is inevitable sooner or later if the candidates are to fulfil their long term goal of joining the euro. \par \par \uc2  Nielsen at Goldman thinks the shift may be a matter of months away in Poland. \par \par \uc2  "My expectation is that Poland will introduce an ERM2 type arrangement, which would make a lot of sense...in the case of Hungary even though the bands are very wide it has helped to guide the market and that has been useful." \par \par \uc2  His view is shared by Charles Robertson, an eastern Europe analyst at ING Barings. \par \par \uc2  "The (Polish) government has put forward proposals to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and many people have assumed it will be an ERM2 type system. \par \par \uc2  "This could come before the end of the year, though technically they could have it in place in days." \par \par \uc2  Robertson and Nielsen both said they would not recommend managed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes for all emerging market countries. \par \par \uc2  However, the EU candidate countries are a special case in that they are embarked on a clearly defined path towards economic convergence with the EU bloc, which would see them benefit from more predictable exchange rate moves. \par \par \uc2  Robertson also said that small, open economies like those in eastern Europe had, in the past, tended to benefit from managed or fixed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  systems. \par \par \uc2  "For small, open economies fixed \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates can be a very good method through which to keep down inflation." \par \par \uc2  Robertson cited the example of Belgium, which closely linked its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to that of Germany before the euro came in. The Baltic states are another case in point - all have enjoyed strong growth rates in a fixed exchange rate environment. \par \par \uc2  OBSTACLES TO CHANGE \par \par \uc2  Some may see managed \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes as inevitable, but there remain persuasive economic arguments against as well as significant political obstacles to change. \par \par \uc2  The Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has so far resisted calls from the government to change the system to weaken the zloty, saying it sees no reason to tamper with the free-float. The regime cannot be changed without the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's assent. \par \par \uc2  Analysts at JPMorgan think the Poles will maintain a free-floating zloty for the foreseeable future. \par \par \uc2  "The whole thing looks to have been a bluff from the government and we don't expect anything to change in the short-term or even the medium-term," said Juliette Declercq, emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JPMorgan. \par \par \uc2  Declercq said Poland was unlikely to have the same problems as the Czech Republic and Hungary, because it doesn't have the same level of foreign direct investment and privatisation inflows forcing up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Once Polish interest rates start to come down, the zloty should start to ease, she added. \par \par \uc2  Regulating exchange rate regimes may help to curb unwanted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength, but can also limit the scope for growth-boosting depreciation. \par \par \uc2  In addition, a flexible exchange rate can be useful in allowing the market to reflect increased perceptions of political and economic risk, as demonstrated by the recent sharp fall of the Slovak crown on pre-election political jitters. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020606dy6600rmq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
