\par\par \b \uc2  Eastern Europe finds  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  pain in investment. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Richard Baum \par \uc2 586 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 1999\par 15:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Dec 15 (Reuters) - A surge in the strength of the Slovakian crown has focussed attention on the downside of big investment flows into emerging markets: an appreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that can squeeze a developing economy's growth. \par \par \uc2  With central banks in several Eastern European countries uncomfortable about overseas investments boosting their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  excessively, there is growing talk of channelling large \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows through special off-market accounts. \par \par \uc2  The idea, discussed by the Polish and Czech central banks and touted as a solution to the recent strengthening of Slovakia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , would be simple enough to implement. \par \par \uc2  The foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  paid by a foreign company for a local business investment would be kept in a special account at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and used either to repay foreign debts or converted gradually into domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent it hitting the market in one go. \par \par \uc2  But whether central banks will go with the idea is a matter of debate among analysts, and pertinent at a time when the United States is urging emerging countries to tackle exchange rate problems before they need to call on the International Monetary Fund. \par \par \uc2  EU TALKS BOOST SLOVAK CROWN \par \par \uc2  The case for Slovakia is perhaps most straightforward. The European Union's decision last week to start membership talks with Slovakia has inflated expectations that foreign businesses will pour money into the country. \par \par \uc2  That in turn has helped boost the crown to record levels against the euro, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday and Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  But \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the trend of the market rarely provides anything other than a short term fix. Nor are attempts to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by cutting interest rates likely to work if most of the inflows are for direct investment, as the Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has discovered this year. \par \par \uc2  So Slovakia might well consider keeping privatisation inflows in a special account and using the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pay off foreign debt, said Stefan Kolek, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HypoVereinsbank. Nevertheless, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will still probably appreciate, he said \par \par \uc2  SMOOTHING OUT THE FLOWS \par \par \uc2  The problem for Poland is that it will need to convert inflows into zlotys to pay for pension reforms, said Banque Paribas economist Ashley Dodd-Noble. That means a privatisation account will only smooth out the flow of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the market, not keep it off altogether. \par \par \uc2  The issue has become less important in recent weeks in Poland because the simple threat of a privatisation account has, along with other factors, weakened the zloty. \par \par \uc2  But Dodd-Noble thinks Poland will at least temporarily use special accounts, for example during the privatisation of telecoms company TPSA in the first quarter of next year. \par \par \uc2  The situation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Czech Republic\b0 \highlight\uc2  is less clear. Analysts say the lack of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the crown broke through 36 to the euro recently reflects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's wariness of acting against the market trend. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has recommended the government channel privatisation revenues into a special account, but the finance ministry wants to use the money for infrastructure and housing projects. \par \par \uc2  The Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be hoping instead that its interest rates are low enough to persuade foreign companies to finance their investments locally, said Barclays Capital strategist Nick Douch. \par \par \uc2  "What we don't know about this money coming in is how much will come from outside or how much will be borrowed locally," he said. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvcf08651\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
