\par\par \b \uc2  C/A deficits take toll on some emerging  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 443 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Mai 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 15 (Reuter) - Exotic \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Thailand and the Czech Republic, countries with high current account deficits, came in to the firing line on Thursday and analysts said heavy selling would persist. \par \par \uc2  Concerns about higher interest rates, both in Japan and the United States, have alarmed investors and has led to heavy selling in Thailand and the Czech Republic as the international cost of funding investments in these countries would rise. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think it's an attack on the Czech Republic or Thailand per se, but the reality is investors who have been running yield positions are closing them out," said Chris Tinker, chief economist at Standard Chartered in London. \par \par \uc2  Market analysts explained that yield hungry investors have funded their positions via cheap yen borrowings over the past year. \par \par \uc2  A rise in the Japanese discount rate, currently at 0.5 percent, would raise the cost of funding these positions and would trigger an unwinding of investments in these countries. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that, if borrowing costs rise in the United States and Japan, investors would become reluctant to finance investments where high currents account deficits are being run, because of their greater dependence of speculative foreign capital. \par \par \uc2  Thailand and the Czech Republic were two obvious candidates where large current account deficits have led to a flight in capital, which resulted in big drops in the baht and and crown today. \par \par \uc2  As a result, the Bank of Thailand, with several Asian central banks, and the Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (CNB), \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier today to defend their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against further speculative attacks. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht was quoted sharply firmer at 25.250/25.350 late on Thursday following the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against 25.910/25.940 early in the morning and 26.330/26.430 late on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Czech crown fell one full percentage point in early Thursday trading to hit a record low five percent weaker than its central target rate against a basket of dollars and marks. It later rebounded to minus 3.4 percent following the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "What is happening in Thailand and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Czech Republic\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in two very different parts of the world, is that current accounts are no longer being forgiven in financial markets and Brazil is vulnerable," said Richard Gray, emerging market analyst at BankAmerica in London. \par \par \uc2  "The spillover effect is being transmitted around the world by fear of current accounts. I do think you may see some knock-on consequences from the Thai baht into the Czech Crown and independently into Brazil," he added. \par \par \uc2  -- Diane Craft, London Newsroom +44 171 542 5110 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt5f05m0n\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (czech republic near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw o